N EXPERIMENTS WITH A DIFFERENT SDG OUTCOME

Here, we show the applicability of our machine learning framework CG-CT in experiments with another SDG outcome. Specifically, to end hunger, we focus on the prevalence of undernourishment (Goal 2, Target 2.1.1) [47]. Hence, in this analysis, we have the following variables: (i) the outcome variable is the relative reduction in the prevalence of undernourishment (measured as percentage of the population); (ii) the treatment variable is the official development aid volume in USD millions per year earmarked to end hunger in each country; and (iii) the country characteristics as covariates. We use the same country characteristics as in our main analysis with HIV infection rates (except that we omitted the prevalence of undernourishment, since it is the outcome in the following analysis). We run the same experiments as in our main analysis, i.e., with both semi-synthetic and real-world data. We again use the same baselines, performance metrics, and semi-synthetic data generation procedure.
Our results (in Table S6) show that our CG-CT provides state-ofthe-art performance in experiments with both semi-synthetic and real-world data. Thereby, we demonstrate the applicability of our machine learning framework to other SDG targets. Altogether, this confirms the large potential of our machine learning framework to predict reliable heterogeneous treatment effects of development aid on different SDG outcomes, thereby promoting progress towards achieving the SDGs by the year 2030.

